Chapter: WebRoute
  cites Works chapter Http
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The part of a web server that has nothing to do with how bytes arrive: the
 request count, the standard endpoints every app gets for free, and the
 dispatch that offers a request to those before the app's own route.

 It is a chapter of its own so that the bare-metal server and the hosted one
 share it rather than each carrying a copy. They cannot share `WebServer`
 itself: that chapter cites `NetIO`, `NetworkStack`, `TcpTransport`, `Tcp`,
 `Ethernet` and `NetDriver`, and a hosted build has none of that hardware
 below it. `Http` and this chapter cite nothing outside the foreword, so both
 servers can reach them.

 THE POINT IS THAT ONE ROUTE FUNCTION SERVES BOTH TARGETS. If the standard
 endpoints lived in only one of the two servers, the same
 `(HttpRequest -> HttpResponse)` would answer /api/health on one target and
 404 on the other, which is the per-target rewrite this split exists to stop.

 We say:

Section: State

  WebState = record { request-count : Integer }

  web-new : WebState
  web-new = WebState { request-count = 0 }

Section: JSON Helpers

  jstr : Text -> Text
  jstr (s) = "\"" & s & "\""

  json-ok : Text -> Text
  json-ok (body) = body

  web-not-found : Text -> Text
  web-not-found (path) = "{\"error\":\"not found\",\"path\":" & jstr path & "}"

Section: HTTP JSON Response

  http-json : Text -> HttpResponse
  http-json (body) = HttpResponse { status = http-200, content-type = "application/json", body = body }

  http-json-cors : Text -> HttpResponse
  http-json-cors (body) = HttpResponse { status = http-200, content-type = "application/json", body = body }

Section: Standard Routes + Dispatch

  web-standard : WebState, Text -> Maybe HttpResponse
  web-standard (st) (path) =
    if path == "/api/health" then Just (http-json "{\"status\":\"ok\"}")
    else if path == "/api/status" then Just (http-json ("{\"requests\":" & show (st.request-count) & ",\"mode\":\"http\"}"))
    else None

 An observer sees EVERY request with the response actually returned, which
 the route cannot: `web-standard` answers `/api/health` and `/api/status`
 before the route is consulted, so an app that logs from inside its own
 route records neither. `web-observe-none` is for an app that wants no log.

  web-observe-none : Text -> Integer
  web-observe-none (line) = 0

  web-dispatch : (HttpRequest -> HttpResponse), (Text -> Integer), WebState, HttpRequest -> HttpResponse
  web-dispatch (route) (obs) (st) (req) =
    let resp = when web-standard st (req.path)
      is Just (std) -> std
      is None -> route req
    in let seen = obs (req.method & " " & req.path & " " & resp.status)
    in resp
